  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 AM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging continues to be the dominant large-scale driver \nof our weather resulting extreme heat. However, the forecast \narea remains on the northern periphery of this ridge, which puts\nmainly northern Missouri, into the path of convection. In the \nmid-levels there is a plume of 14+C air at 700mb, extending from\nsouthern Kansas through northwestern Missouri. Convection is \nhaving a difficult time building eastward into that warm mid- \nlevel air. At the surface, a boundary extends eastward along the\nHighway 36 corridor with a pool of low-level moisture in the \nvicinity of that boundary. This moistures is very shallow and \nbased of the latest aircraft soundings into MCI, there is ample \ndry air aloft. Given the warm air aloft, the chance for \nconvection to occur along and south roughly a Kansas City to \nMoberly is unlikely (<25%). For areas north of this, the \npotential is greater and have generally arranged PoPs with this \nin mind, with the greatest chances (20-35%) across the northern\nportions of the forecast area. Instability will again be strong\nto extreme across much of northern Missouri with 3500 to 4500 \nJ/kg of SBCAPE. Stronger flow further north may also lead to \nincreasing shear with northward extent. Additionally, a fairly \nrobust shortwave/ internal PV anomaly, likely from the deep \nconvection over southern Nebraska and northern Kansas currently,\nwill be moving into this airmass later today. Given this, we \nmay see more robust/ potentially severe convection across \nnorthern Missouri this afternoon and evening. \n\nWith regards to the heat for today, another day of high temperatures \nin the middle to upper 90s can be expected south of a St Joseph\nto Moberly line. A few areas in our south will also have \npotential to see 100+ readings. In fact, most of Linn County, KS\nhas a greater than 50% chance of seeing 100+ degree \ntemperatures, with chances as high as 10-20% into the KC Metro.\nWith dewpoints ranging from the lower 70s in our south, the \nupper 70s, across northern Missouri, heat index values will once\nagain be pushing 105-115, generally along and south of the \nHighway 36 corridor. The current heat headlines look to be in a\ngood place and given the likelihood of seeing another day of \nheat have extended the Excessive Heat Warning and the Heat \nAdvisory into Saturday. It's possible these will need to be \nadjusted depending on how the forecast plays out but confidence \nwas high enough to extend the current headlines. \n\nA cold front will move southward through the area Sunday and that \nshould help to finally ease the extreme heat that has been affecting \nthe area. By Monday, temperatures should be about 10 degrees cooler \nthan the highs for Saturday. But dewpoints will also be 5-10 degrees \ncooler as well. Beyond Monday, temperatures are more likely to be \ncloser to normal than to be well above normal like we saw this week. \nEnsemble mean 500mb heights show the upper ridge suppressed and \ncentered over the Southwestern US. However, there are some \nensemble clusters (about 25-30% of members) that do have a \nstronger and more Plains oriented ridge axis. So there is some \npotential for temperatures to trend warmer as the forecast \nevolves and updates with time.",
